WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.573 --> 00:00:03.573
(light soft music)

00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:09.582
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:00:20.905 --> 00:00:23.655
(dramatic music)

00:00:25.593 --> 00:00:29.676
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:01:17.281 --> 00:01:21.448
- In 2001, the term femicide
emerged as a way to describe

00:01:22.389 --> 00:01:26.084
violence against women
because they are women.

00:01:26.084 --> 00:01:27.985
When thinking about femicide,

00:01:27.985 --> 00:01:29.557
the first thing that we want to remember

00:01:29.557 --> 00:01:32.475
is that femicide is a global phenomenon.

00:01:32.475 --> 00:01:35.771
Femicide is not unique towards Mexico,

00:01:35.771 --> 00:01:39.938
however right now since 2004,
the femicides have increased.

00:01:41.252 --> 00:01:44.169
(light soft music)

00:01:49.195 --> 00:01:53.278
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:02:31.653 --> 00:02:32.989
One thing I try to do in my work

00:02:32.989 --> 00:02:35.705
is to talk about critically
what are the conditions

00:02:35.705 --> 00:02:37.641
that give rise to femicide?

00:02:37.641 --> 00:02:40.224
(upbeat music)

00:02:52.842 --> 00:02:56.234
Businesses don't want
to come and set up shop

00:02:56.234 --> 00:02:57.839
if there is negative attention.

00:02:57.839 --> 00:02:59.815
So the government talks about femicide,

00:02:59.815 --> 00:03:02.146
the killing of young
women and girls in Mexico,

00:03:02.146 --> 00:03:04.128
as a dark legend.

00:03:04.128 --> 00:03:06.445
The activists and the mothers will not

00:03:06.445 --> 00:03:08.921
let this escape public attention.

00:03:08.921 --> 00:03:11.214
One of the ways that mothers
and activists do this

00:03:11.214 --> 00:03:13.045
is by painting crosses.

00:03:13.045 --> 00:03:15.962
(light soft music)

00:03:17.575 --> 00:03:21.658
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:03:42.273 --> 00:03:45.176
So when you look at the
disappeared women in Juarez,

00:03:45.176 --> 00:03:46.700
these are not the affluent.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.341
These are typically poor women.

00:03:49.341 --> 00:03:51.194
When the free trade agreements happened,

00:03:51.194 --> 00:03:55.958
most of the rural areas of
southern Mexico were decimated.

00:03:55.958 --> 00:03:59.197
So what happened is that
workers in the southern states

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:01.394
of Mexico were forced to migrate

00:04:01.394 --> 00:04:04.063
to look for work in the factory settings.

00:04:04.063 --> 00:04:06.527
If a woman disappears in Ciudad Juarez,

00:04:06.527 --> 00:04:10.486
there are often no family
members there to look for them.

00:04:10.486 --> 00:04:13.365
(light soft music)

00:04:13.365 --> 00:04:17.448
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:04:48.026 --> 00:04:51.850
We know that patriarchy is
again a global phenomenon.

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:53.257
But we also want to think about

00:04:53.257 --> 00:04:55.044
what the conditions are in Mexico.

00:04:55.044 --> 00:04:57.046
Finding these bodies is typically

00:04:57.046 --> 00:04:58.799
done by the family members.

00:04:58.799 --> 00:05:01.716
(light soft music)

00:05:03.463 --> 00:05:06.455
State officials have not been
investigating these crimes.

00:05:06.455 --> 00:05:07.753
Often the government would say,

00:05:07.753 --> 00:05:09.103
"She's out with a boy".

00:05:09.103 --> 00:05:10.903
"She's out on the wrong side of town."

00:05:10.903 --> 00:05:13.273
"She was a prostitute, she was drinking."

00:05:13.273 --> 00:05:16.226
They articulate the women as
leading these double lives.

00:05:16.226 --> 00:05:18.817
In my interviews with several
of the family members,

00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:21.629
after the femicides
occurred, many of the men

00:05:21.629 --> 00:05:24.812
in relationships would say
to their woman comments like,

00:05:24.812 --> 00:05:28.457
"If you act out, if you start
showing your independence,

00:05:28.457 --> 00:05:31.264
"you too are going to end up a victim."

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:34.369
(light soft music)

00:05:34.369 --> 00:05:36.763
The role of the state has been taken on

00:05:36.763 --> 00:05:39.727
by the mothers of the
victims and the activists.

00:05:39.727 --> 00:05:44.123
They have been for years
holding restreos, or searches,

00:05:44.123 --> 00:05:46.868
out in the desert to look for remains.

00:05:46.868 --> 00:05:49.857
That creates a double trauma
because the initial trauma

00:05:49.857 --> 00:05:53.368
was the femicide, but the
trauma then that is taken on

00:05:53.368 --> 00:05:55.845
by the family members
having to go and search

00:05:55.845 --> 00:05:58.100
for their loved ones, search for clothing,

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:01.448
search for bones in the desert,
is another set of trauma.

00:06:01.448 --> 00:06:04.927
And these restreos are doing
the work of the government.

00:06:04.927 --> 00:06:09.010
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:41.038
And so one of the things

00:06:41.038 --> 00:06:43.822
we want to consider is who is culpable.

00:06:43.822 --> 00:06:46.739
(light soft music)

00:06:47.897 --> 00:06:50.188
There are many activists
who talk about the fact

00:06:50.188 --> 00:06:53.595
that American citizens can
be crossing into Juarez

00:06:53.595 --> 00:06:55.129
and targeting the women.

00:06:55.129 --> 00:06:57.931
One man that I interviewed
was working in Juarez

00:06:57.931 --> 00:07:00.737
as an investigative journalist
and he said that many

00:07:00.737 --> 00:07:03.841
of the men that he would interview
that crossed from El Paso

00:07:03.841 --> 00:07:06.720
would tell him, if you want
to rape and murder a woman,

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.774
there is no easier place
than Cuidad Juarez.

00:07:09.774 --> 00:07:11.997
And that is because of the impunity.

00:07:11.997 --> 00:07:14.747
(dramatic music)

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:22.052
No one is held responsible.

00:07:22.052 --> 00:07:25.145
The government doesn't
want the public to think

00:07:25.145 --> 00:07:28.671
that these women were real
and therefore crimes continue.

00:07:28.671 --> 00:07:30.687
And that then feeds into a culture.

00:07:30.687 --> 00:07:34.770
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:08:14.262 --> 00:08:16.511
Typically one thing
that people want to know

00:08:16.511 --> 00:08:19.297
is how can we address
the issue of femicide.

00:08:19.297 --> 00:08:20.792
What can we do?

00:08:20.792 --> 00:08:24.085
One of the ideas was to boycott
some of the corporations

00:08:24.085 --> 00:08:26.085
that own the mahiladoras.

00:08:26.085 --> 00:08:28.112
However when speaking with the workers,

00:08:28.112 --> 00:08:30.119
they asked individuals not to boycott

00:08:30.119 --> 00:08:33.113
because that would then
mean a job loss for them.

00:08:33.113 --> 00:08:35.834
So it's very important, the
first thing I always say

00:08:35.834 --> 00:08:38.540
is we need to speak with the
community members themselves.

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:40.296
The community members are the ones

00:08:40.296 --> 00:08:42.373
embodying and living this reality.

00:08:42.373 --> 00:08:43.764
They are the ones that are on

00:08:43.764 --> 00:08:46.016
the front lines engaging the activism.

00:08:46.016 --> 00:08:48.555
Our role is to act in solidarity with them

00:08:48.555 --> 00:08:50.801
in whatever capacity that we can.

00:08:50.801 --> 00:08:52.439
But we follow their lead.

00:08:52.439 --> 00:08:54.243
Projects like the Faces of Femicide

00:08:54.243 --> 00:08:56.063
mural are extremely important.

00:08:56.063 --> 00:08:59.048
What the Faces of Femicide
does with the work

00:08:59.048 --> 00:09:02.292
of Juvia and Miclavio
is interview the mothers

00:09:02.292 --> 00:09:03.913
about their daughters lives.

00:09:03.913 --> 00:09:04.922
What were their dreams?

00:09:04.922 --> 00:09:06.247
What were their aspirations?

00:09:06.247 --> 00:09:07.624
What were they studying?

00:09:07.624 --> 00:09:09.331
What did they want to accomplish?

00:09:09.331 --> 00:09:12.945
To show the world that this
was an actual human being

00:09:12.945 --> 00:09:14.683
and the government cannot erase

00:09:14.683 --> 00:09:16.892
or engage in cultural amnesia.

00:09:16.892 --> 00:09:20.975
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:09:55.126 --> 00:09:57.477
One of the things that's also
really important to remember

00:09:57.477 --> 00:10:00.784
is that we cannot engage in stereotyping.

00:10:00.784 --> 00:10:03.558
And currently what's going on
with the Trump administration

00:10:03.558 --> 00:10:07.235
is it functions to just fuel
those negative stereotypes.

00:10:07.235 --> 00:10:10.237
Mexicans as rapists,
Mexicans as criminals.

00:10:10.237 --> 00:10:13.901
These poor brown women, no,
these are women with agency.

00:10:13.901 --> 00:10:16.222
These are women leading social movements.

00:10:16.222 --> 00:10:18.354
These are women with unique stories.

00:10:18.354 --> 00:10:20.599
Rape against women is a global phenomenon.

00:10:20.599 --> 00:10:22.515
Gendered violence is a global phenomenon.

00:10:22.515 --> 00:10:24.957
This is in no way, shape or form

00:10:24.957 --> 00:10:26.732
a Mexican problem or something

00:10:26.732 --> 00:10:29.214
inherent to a Mexican sensibility.

00:10:29.214 --> 00:10:33.297
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:11:13.613 --> 00:11:16.613
(light soft music)

